Fix a Quickshell buffer-manager deadlock that silently disables ScreencopyView
for the entire life of a shell process.

WlBufferManager::initWindow() needs a window with a live scene graph to query
render formats. When the caller hands it one that is null or not yet
initialised, it defers: it registers a callback via
ProxiedWindow::callOnScenegraphInit() and returns. That queue is only ever
drained by ProxiedWindow::onSceneGraphInitialized(), which runs from
QQuickWindow::sceneGraphInitialized -- a signal each window emits exactly once,
when it first comes online. So the deferral terminates only if another window
is still to be created. In a shell whose windows are already up, nothing emits
it again and the wait never ends.

ScreencopyView::componentComplete() passes QQuickItem::window(), which is null
for any item that is not in a scene. That is not an exotic state: an
Instantiator delegate created on demand and reparented into place afterwards
has no window at componentComplete, and that is exactly how a lock screen's
per-output capture pool is built.

The two combine into a permanent latch. The first such ScreencopyView sets the
function-static `initWaiting` and registers the callback that will never fire;
every later view hits `if (initWaiting) return;` and does not even re-register.
mRenderFormatsReady stays false, ready() is never emitted,
ScreencopyView::onBuffersReady() never runs, and createContext() is never
called. The client issues no screencopy protocol at all -- it does not even
bind ext_output_image_capture_source_manager_v1 -- so there is nothing to see
on the wire and no error anywhere. hasContent simply stays false forever.

Observed as: a workspace-backdrop lock screen that shows only the fallback
wallpaper on every lock, on a freshly started shell, until the config is
reloaded. Reloading fixes it because tearing down and recreating every window
makes sceneGraphInitialized fire again, which drains the stale callback.

Confirmed mechanically: an unparented ScreencopyView created 1s after startup
stays at hasContent=false indefinitely, and creating an unrelated PanelWindow
1.5s later makes it capture immediately with nothing else touching it.

Any window with a live scene graph can supply the render formats, and the
non-deferred path already calls initRenderFormats() from the GUI thread, so
this looks for an existing window before deciding to wait for a new one.

Applies to Quickshell v0.3.0; unfixed on master as of 28771c7.
---
diff --git a/src/wayland/buffer/manager.cpp b/src/wayland/buffer/manager.cpp
--- a/src/wayland/buffer/manager.cpp
+++ b/src/wayland/buffer/manager.cpp
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@

 #include <drm_fourcc.h>
 #include <qdebug.h>
+#include <qguiapplication.h>
 #include <qlogging.h>
 #include <qloggingcategory.h>
 #include <qmatrix4x4.h>
@@ -65,6 +66,24 @@ void WlBufferManager::initWindow(QQuickWindow* window) {
 	if (this->p->mRenderFormatsReady) return;
 	qCDebug(logBuffer) << "Initializing buffer manager";

+	// Callers do not always have a window of their own. ScreencopyView passes
+	// QQuickItem::window(), which is null for an item that is not in a scene --
+	// an Instantiator delegate that gets reparented into place later, for
+	// instance. Deferring to the next sceneGraphInitialized only helps while
+	// windows are still being created: once a shell's windows are up, nothing
+	// emits that signal again, so the wait below never ends and the manager
+	// never becomes ready. Any live window can supply the render formats, so
+	// look for one before deciding to wait for a new one.
+	if (!window || !window->isSceneGraphInitialized()) {
+		for (auto* topLevel: QGuiApplication::allWindows()) {
+			auto* quickWindow = qobject_cast<QQuickWindow*>(topLevel);
+			if (quickWindow != nullptr && quickWindow->isSceneGraphInitialized()) {
+				window = quickWindow;
+				break;
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	static bool initWaiting = false;
 	if (!window || !window->isSceneGraphInitialized()) {
 		if (initWaiting) return;
